commit e8c0dfe49828e02076f4790aaf801d9f36f98664
Author: Erik Sipsma <erik@sipsma.dev>
Date:   Fri Jun 26 13:15:14 2026 -0700

    fix(wcprof): zero-dur wait-target wrong answer; hard-fail fallback anchors
    
    Two items from the re-confirm review.
    
    (1) Showstopper found by fresh Codex, verified on the harness: a zero-duration
    child whose end coincides with a gating wait's end was anchored EARLY. joinUpTo
    runs before the action tie-break, so it grabbed the unstarted child at the
    pre-gate clock before the wait's own gate raised the clock. For a leaf that was
    benign (the child's finish is absorbed), but when the child is itself a WAIT
    TARGET the wrong-early finish propagates: P waits T (gating to 100) then spawns
    zero-dur Z at 100; B waits Z; A waits B then runs 200ms self — Z anchored at
    P's pre-wait clock (50) gives B=50, A=250, makespan 250 instead of 300, with NO
    FallbackAnchor to flag it. (My earlier "benign" call covered only the leaf.)
    
    Fix, two parts:
      - joinUpTo no longer anchors an unstarted child whose own spawn is still
        pending at this instant (the only way an in-range child is unstarted there:
        a zero-duration child whose spawn and end both equal the gate time). It
        defers, so the gate raises the clock first and the spawn anchors the child.
      - actionRank now orders spawn BEFORE self (gate -> spawn -> self), so the
        deferred child's spawn anchors it concurrently with a same-time self segment
        rather than serialized after it. Only a zero-duration child can share a
        spawn instant with a self-segment start (a normal child's interval carves
        the self out of that point), so this rank change touches nothing else.
    Both faces are tested (wait-target propagation, self-segment start). The fix
    also eliminates the benign zero-dur SimStartConflict, making that signal clean:
    a conflict now implies a recorded-offset fallback.
    
    (2) Per Erik's decision, ANY recorded-offset fallback anchor now hard-fails the
    OTel structural gate (was report-only). A fallback is the surviving startOf
    approximation -- the replay could not compute an op from causal prefixes -- and
    a finite what-if sweep cannot prove the resulting rankings sound, so the gate
    enforces on the precondition. MaxFallbackAnchors becomes the explicit opt-out
    (tolerate up to N) for best-effort offline analysis. 0 on all current traces,
    so it is free here; the cross-root shape that still produces fallbacks is
    item-3 (on-demand root scheduling) territory.
    
    Real traces bit-unchanged (native -0.1% / OTel -2.4%); full suite + the
    gate-using emit-path tests green.
    
    Signed-off-by: Erik Sipsma <erik@sipsma.dev>

